WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.489 --> 00:00:04.370
Continuity in ​Avengers: Endgame​ could
get confusing... even if you were in charge

00:00:04.370 --> 00:00:05.760
of decorating the sets.

00:00:06.090 --> 00:00:11.140
A sharp-eyed fan has pointed out an inconsistency
when the Avengers travel back to 2012 — one

00:00:11.140 --> 00:00:14.780
you're only likely to notice if you have every
detail of ​The Avengers​ committed to

00:00:14.780 --> 00:00:15.280
memory.

00:00:15.500 --> 00:00:19.580
In case you need a quick refresher: Endgame's
second act sees different teams of Avengers

00:00:19.599 --> 00:00:24.160
traveling to different periods in the past,
in an attempt to snatch all six Infinity Stones

00:00:24.160 --> 00:00:26.540
before the Mad Titan Thanos can get his hands
on them.

00:00:26.849 --> 00:00:30.880
Tony Stark, Steve Rogers, Scott Lang, and
Bruce Banner are tasked with returning to

00:00:30.880 --> 00:00:35.410
2012 and the Battle of New York, where they
know three can be found: the Time Stone, the

00:00:35.410 --> 00:00:36.960
Mind Stone, and the Space Stone.

00:00:37.290 --> 00:00:40.940
Banner is able to convince the Ancient One
to pony up the Time Stone, but grabbing the

00:00:40.940 --> 00:00:43.420
other two proves to be a great deal trickier.

00:00:43.660 --> 00:00:48.210
After a brawl in Stark Tower, Loki is initially
secured, and both the Space Stone and the

00:00:48.210 --> 00:00:50.520
Mind Stone is in the custody of the Avengers.

00:00:50.960 --> 00:00:54.460
But as they and a bunch of S.H.I.E.L.D. agents
start heading down the elevator to drop Loki

00:00:54.460 --> 00:00:58.120
off to the authorities, it becomes apparent
that one of the heroes will have to take

00:00:58.160 --> 00:00:58.660
the stairs.

00:00:59.620 --> 00:01:00.180
"Woah!"

00:01:01.400 --> 00:01:03.960
"What do you think?  Maximum occupancy has been reached."

00:01:04.080 --> 00:01:04.880
"Take the stairs!"

00:01:05.840 --> 00:01:06.720
"Stop Stop!"

00:01:08.520 --> 00:01:12.460
What we'd like to call your attention to is
the interior of that elevator, because when

00:01:12.460 --> 00:01:15.999
the time-traveling Avengers take a ride in
it, it looks a little bit different than it

00:01:16.000 --> 00:01:17.120
did the first time we saw it.

00:01:17.700 --> 00:01:21.160
Here's the Stark Tower elevator as we saw
it in 2012's The Avengers.

00:01:21.280 --> 00:01:25.560
Meanwhile, here is the same elevator, with
all of our heroes aboard, in Avengers: Endgame.

00:01:25.840 --> 00:01:29.810
We never realized that one of the lesser-known
effects of time travel involved slight changes

00:01:29.810 --> 00:01:31.760
to the decor of your target destination.

00:01:32.380 --> 00:01:34.840
You'll notice that the rest of the set dressing
is spot on.

00:01:35.020 --> 00:01:38.360
The interior of the elevator, though, is plainly
not even in the ballpark.

00:01:39.140 --> 00:01:42.780
We know what you're thinking: the hardworking
folks behind Avengers: Endgame's set design

00:01:42.780 --> 00:01:44.000
deserve a break here.

00:01:44.210 --> 00:01:47.740
After all, they were tawsked not only with
giving a distinct look and feel to a film

00:01:47.740 --> 00:01:51.920
that would become the biggest of all time,
but also with painstakingly replicating sets

00:01:51.920 --> 00:01:55.490
from previous movies, ones which they almost
certainly didn't even work on.

00:01:55.580 --> 00:01:59.380
How could they possibly have been expected
to nail every last little detail?

00:01:59.580 --> 00:02:01.540
Well, realistically, they couldn't have.

00:02:02.020 --> 00:02:06.299
But there's a flip side to that argument:
obviously, The Avengers was used as a reference

00:02:06.300 --> 00:02:07.460
when staging this scene.

00:02:07.720 --> 00:02:10.600
If it hadn't been, the rest of the decor in
the shot wouldn't have matched up.

00:02:10.920 --> 00:02:14.640
It's highly unlikely that that table, those
lamps, and that sculpture had been sitting

00:02:14.640 --> 00:02:18.760
in a closet somewhere on Disney's lot since
The Avengers completed filming, just waiting

00:02:18.760 --> 00:02:20.920
to be dragged out and used in another scene.

00:02:20.980 --> 00:02:25.540
No, those props almost certainly had to be
recreated for Endgame — and if that detail

00:02:25.540 --> 00:02:29.240
of the old Stark Tower set was subject to
scrutiny when the flick's crew was building

00:02:29.240 --> 00:02:32.080
the sets for the Battle of New York scenes,
then all of them were.

00:02:32.400 --> 00:02:33.720
So, what's up with the elevator?

00:02:34.300 --> 00:02:36.000
Did they think nobody would ever notice?

00:02:36.460 --> 00:02:37.320
"Thought we wouldn't notice.

00:02:38.440 --> 00:02:39.020
But we did."

00:02:39.220 --> 00:02:41.760
We'd like to propose an in-universe explanation.

00:02:42.120 --> 00:02:46.480
What if the Avengers didn't travel back to
their Battle of New York, but an ever-so-slightly

00:02:46.480 --> 00:02:47.940
different, alternate Battle of New York?

00:02:48.200 --> 00:02:52.340
It could even be that the aesthetic of Tony
Stark's elevator was literally the only difference

00:02:52.340 --> 00:02:53.620
between the two timelines.

00:02:54.020 --> 00:02:58.000
We like this explanation, because maintaining
continuity with the entire rest of the Marvel

00:02:58.000 --> 00:03:01.960
Cinematic Universe on Endgame must have been
the ultimate logistical nightmare.

00:03:02.360 --> 00:03:05.980
It definitely couldn't have been a snap to
bring everything back from previous films.

00:03:06.340 --> 00:03:10.750
There are probably a million other tiny errors
like this just waiting to be discovered, and

00:03:10.750 --> 00:03:13.880
we just explained every last one of them in
one fell swoop.

